 {"attempt_id": "C0047-A001", "author": "claude-probability-1000-continue-0907", "independent_check": "", "key_identity": "", "method": "Attempt the non-amenable direction through the spectral radius criterion of Benjamini-Schramm and Hutchcroft's p_c <= 1/spectral radius... type bounds.", "obstruction": "Need p_c(G) < 1/rho(G) or another uniform criterion for every Cayley graph of every non-amenable group.", "problem_id": "C0047", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T06:38:45Z", "result": "Known route: if p_c < 1/rho where rho is the spectral radius of simple random walk, then at p slightly above p_c the expected number of open paths of length n to distance n grows slower than the number of returns... this gives p_c < p_u for graphs where p_c(G) < 1/(rho ... ) and is the content of the Benjamini-Schramm 1996 criterion p_c < 1/(1 + spectral gap)?; the general conjecture needs p_c < p_u even when p_c is close to 1/rho... For groups with p_c(G) < 1/rho(G) the conjecture holds; the remaining case is groups with a Cayley graph where p_c >= 1/rho, and the Pak-Smirnova trick replaces the generating set to make p_c small relative to rho. So the conjecture in its generating-set-free form (some Cayley graph) is settled by Pak-Smirnova, while the conjecture for EVERY Cayley graph reduces to showing p_c(G) < 1/rho(G)^{?}... I checked the inequality p_c <= 1/(1 + h) where h is the Cheeger constant... Benjamini-Schramm prove p_c <= 1/(1+h) and p_u >= 1/(1+h)?? no. The honest reduction: for every Cayley graph, p_c < p_u would follow from p_c < 1/rho (Benjamini-Schramm criterion) and the open question is whether every non-amenable Cayley graph satisfies p_c < 1/rho, which fails? For the free group with the standard generators p_c = 1/(2d-1) and 1/rho = (2d-1)/(2 sqrt(2d-1)) > p_c, consistent. No counterexample and no proof.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true}
+{"schema":"probability-attempt.v1","problem_id":"C0047","attempt_id":"C0047-A001-CORR-W560","corrects_attempt_id":"C0047-A001","author":"w560","recorded_at":"2026-10-10T07:23:56.188752+00:00","method":"Primary-source correction and two independent referee boundary checks","key_identity":"D*rho(G,S)*p_c(G,S)<1 with D=|S| and rho the normalized simple-random-walk spectral radius","result":"The older C0047-A001 phrase p_c<1/rho omits D. Since nonamenable Cayley graphs have rho<1 and p_c<=1, that phrase is automatic and cannot prove p_c<p_u. Juschenko-Nagnibeda Section 1 Theorem 2 equation (3) states the sufficient condition rho*p_c*|S|<1. It is not necessary: on the standard 4-regular tree p_c=1/3, p_u=1, rho=sqrt(3)/2, so D*rho*p_c=2sqrt(3)/3>1. The C_2*C_3 triangle-block graph gives a second obstruction with D*rho*p_c>1.26.","obstruction":"Corrected spectral criterion is sufficient only; no parent-problem proof follows. Recent 2026 bond manuscript claims a full solution, independently unverified here; original site formulation is separate.","independent_check":"w558-C0 and w559-source-002 agree on primary formula; w560 checked both cited formula and explicit boundary examples. Served-model/account independence unverified.","source_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/1206.2183v3","substantive":true}
 {"attempt_id": "C0048-A001", "author": "claude-probability-1000-continue-0907", "independent_check": "", "key_identity": "", "method": "Examine whether the reflection-positivity chessboard argument can be pushed down from q >= q_c(d) large to q = 3 in d = 3 using improved contour entropy bounds.", "obstruction": "Contour-entropy constants are far from sharp and the two-dimensional transfer-matrix route has no three-dimensional analogue.", "problem_id": "C0048", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T06:38:45Z", "result": "The Kotecky-Shlosman argument proves a first-order transition when the entropy of contours (bounded by e^{c |contour|}) is beaten by the energy gain, which requires q large enough that log q exceeds a lattice-dependent constant; in d = 3 the best constants give q >= some number in the tens. Improving to q = 3 needs the exact contour entropy, which is not available; numerically the d = 3, q = 3 transition is weakly first order with a tiny latent heat, so any argument must be sharp to within a few percent, beyond what chessboard estimates give. Alternative route through the random-cluster representation: discontinuity is equivalent to non-uniqueness of the infinite-volume random-cluster measure at p_c, i.e. to the wired measure having an infinite cluster at p_c while the free one does not; for q > 4 in d = 2 this was done via the six-vertex transfer matrix, a planar tool. No d = 3 substitute. No result.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true}
